  "slug": "age-dependent-huntington-7aeb",
  "summary": "Eight preprints use cell-type, age and stage-resolved profiling to place synaptic, proteostatic and RNA-binding protein changes in neurodegenerative disease models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is resolving disease-associated molecular change by cell type, age or disease stage. In R6/1 mice, proteomics shows synaptic protein loss in excitatory neurons and reduced glial neuronal-support proteins before motor onset, while TgHD minipig putamen shows age-dependent transcript and protein changes, including reduced D2 neuron markers. Fmr1-KO profiling reports cell type-specific remodelling of excitatory and inhibitory synapses. In ALS-related work, a TDP-43 APEX2 atlas finds interactome changes persisting beyond stress granule clearance, somatic mutation burden is higher in younger ALS donors with an SBS5 signature, and SETX loss has stage-dependent effects in iPSC motor neurons. A multiplexed assay maps GBA1 variant function, and Ankle2-VRK1 work links proteostasis to neuronal resilience in aging Drosophila. Not all items address aging.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
